questions.q:
Match List-I with List-II :
$\begin{array}{|c|c|}
\hline \begin{array}{l} 
\text{List-I} \\
\text{(Defects)}
\end{array} & \begin{array}{l}
\begin{array}{l} 
\text{List-II} \\
\text{(Shown by)}
\end{array}
\end{array} \\
\hline \begin{array}{l} 
\text{(a) Frenkel defect}
\end{array} & \begin{array}{l} 
\text{(i) non-ionic solids and} \\
\text{density of the solid} \\
\text{decreases}
\end{array} \\
\hline \begin{array}{l} 
\text{(b) Schottky defect}
\end{array} & \begin{array}{l} 
\text{(ii) non-ionic solids and} \\
\text{density of the solid} \\
\text{increases}
\end{array} \\
\hline \text{(c) Vacancy defect} & \begin{array}{l} 
\text{(iii) ionic solids and} \\
\text{density of the solid} \\
\text{decreases}
\end{array} \\
\hline \begin{array}{l} 
\text{(d) Interstitial defect}
\end{array} & \begin{array}{l} 
\text{(iv) ionic solids and} \\
\text{density of the solid} \\
\text{remains constant.}
\end{array} \\
\hline
\end{array}$
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :

questions.solution:
Given, cell is fcc, so $Z=4$ Edge length, $a=404 \mathrm{pm}=4.04 \times 10^{-8} \mathrm{~cm}$ Density of metal, $d=2.72 \mathrm{~g} \mathrm{~cm}^{-3}N_A=6.02 \times 10^{23} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$
Molar mass of the metal, $M=$ ?
We know that
density, $d=\frac{Z \times M}{2^3 \cdot N_A}$
\begin{aligned}
\therefore M & =\frac{d \cdot a^3 \cdot N_A}{Z} \\
& =\frac{2.72 \times\left(4.04 \times 10^{-8}\right)^3 \times 6.02 \times 10^{23}}{4} \\
& =27 \mathrm{~g} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1}
\end{aligned}

For body centred cubic (bcc) lattice, distance between two oppositely charged ions,

\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{d} & =\frac{\sqrt{3} \mathrm{a}}{2}=\frac{\sqrt{3} \times 387}{2} \mathrm{pm} \\
& =335.15 \mathrm{pm}
\end{aligned}

Key Idea Apply Kepler's law of area of planetary motion.
The line joining the sun to the planet sweeps out equal areas in equal time interval $i e$, areal velocity is constant.

\frac{\mathrm{dA}}{\mathrm{dt}}=\text { constant }

or

\frac{\mathrm{A}_1}{\mathrm{t}_1}=\frac{\mathrm{A}_2}{\mathrm{t}_2}

\begin{array}{llrl}
\Rightarrow & \mathrm{t}_1 & =\frac{\mathrm{A}_1}{\mathrm{~A}_2} \mathrm{t}_2 \\
\text { Given } & \mathrm{A}_1 & =2 \mathrm{~A}_2 \\
\therefore & \mathrm{t}_1 & =2 \mathrm{t}_2
\end{array}

In case of body centred cubic (bcc) crystal, $\mathrm{a} \sqrt{3}=4 \mathrm{r}$
Hence, atomic radius of lithium, $\mathrm{r}=\frac{\mathrm{a} \sqrt{3}}{4}\begin{aligned}
& =\frac{351 \times 1.732}{4} \\
& =151.98 \mathrm{pm}
\end{aligned}$

Let, in the given crystal $\mathrm{Ni}_{0.98} \mathrm{O}$
\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}=\mathrm{x} \text { and } \mathrm{Ni}^{3+}=0.98-\mathrm{x}

Total charge on $\mathrm{M}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{M}^{3+}$
\begin{aligned}
& =(+2) \mathrm{x}+(+3)(0.98-\mathrm{x}) \\
& =2 \mathrm{x}+2.94-3 \mathrm{x} \\
& =2.94-\mathrm{x}
\end{aligned}

As metal oxide is neutral. Therefore, total charge on cations = total charge on anions.

\begin{aligned}
2.94-\mathrm{x} & =2 \quad[\because \text { Charge of oxygen atom }=-2] \\
\mathrm{x} & =2.94-2=0.94
\end{aligned}

So, the fraction of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions in the crystal

=\frac{0.94}{0.98}=0.96

\begin{aligned}
\frac{\sqrt{3} \mathbf{a}}{2} & =3.7 Å \\
\Rightarrow \quad \mathbf{a} & =\frac{2 \times 3.7}{\sqrt{3}}=4.3 Å
\end{aligned}

Neighbour distance of a body centred cubic cell $\mathrm{d}=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \mathrm{a}$, where $a$ is the lattice parameter.

\begin{array}{ll}
\Rightarrow & 3.7=\frac{\sqrt{3} \mathrm{a}}{2} \\
\text { or } & \mathrm{a}=\frac{2 \times 3.7}{\sqrt{3}}=4.3 Å
\end{array}

Given $2 \pi=2.54 Å$
\Rightarrow \quad r=\frac{2.54}{2}=1.27 Å

For fcc lattice:

\begin{aligned}
r & =\frac{a}{2 \sqrt{2}} \\
a & =r 2 \sqrt{2} \\
& =1.27 \times 2 \times 1.44 \\
& =3.59 \mathrm{~A}^{\circ}
\end{aligned}

For an $A B_2$ type structure, the equivalent equation at equilibrium is

A B_2 \rightleftharpoons A^{2+}+2 B^{-}

For $\mathrm{CaF}_2$, the equivalent equation at equilibrium is

\mathrm{CaF}_2 \rightleftharpoons \mathrm{Ca}^{2+}+2 \mathrm{~F}^{-} \text {. }

Therefore $\mathrm{CaF}_2$ has an $A B_2$ type structure.
